window.B4COURSE = {"title": "Block 4 Academy — Electricity & Electronics", "subtitle": "Fundamentals of Electricity & Electronics · FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.12", "passMark": 0.7, "storeKey": "block4-academy-v1", "modules": [{"n": 1, "day": "Unit 1", "title": "Electricity Basics: Charge, Ohm's Law & Resistors", "ref": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.12 · Fundamentals of electricity", "sections": [{"id": "b4m1s1", "title": "Matter, atoms & electron theory", "lesson": "\n<p><b>The Big Idea.</b> Everything around you is made of <b>matter</b>, and matter\nis built from tiny building blocks called <b>atoms</b>. Every atom has a center\n(the <b>nucleus</b>) packed with <b>protons</b> (positive, +) and <b>neutrons</b>\n(no charge), and a swarm of <b>electrons</b> (negative, −) orbiting around it.</p>\n<p><b>Analogy.</b> Picture the atom as a tiny solar system: the nucleus is the Sun,\nthe electrons are planets zipping around it. The electrons farthest out — in the\n<b>valence</b> (outer) shell — are held the loosest. If an atom has <b>fewer than 4</b>\nvalence electrons, those electrons come loose easily, which makes the material a\ngood <b>conductor</b>. If it has <b>more than 4</b>, they're locked tight, making an\n<b>insulator</b>.</p>\n<h3>Charge and static electricity</h3>\n<p>Normally an atom has equal + and −, so it's balanced (neutral). Rub two things\ntogether and electrons can jump from one to the other. The one that <b>loses</b>\nelectrons becomes <b>positive</b>; the one that <b>gains</b> them becomes\n<b>negative</b>. That stored, non-moving charge is <b>static electricity</b>.\nThe rule to memorize: <b>like charges repel, unlike charges attract.</b></p>\n<h3>What \"voltage\" and \"current\" really mean</h3>\n<p><b>Voltage (EMF)</b> is the electrical \"push\" — the pressure that wants to move\nelectrons. Measured in <b>volts (V)</b>. <b>Current</b> is the actual flow of\nelectrons, measured in <b>amperes (amps, A)</b>. One amp = one <b>coulomb</b> of\ncharge (6.28 × 10¹⁸ electrons) passing a point in one second.</p>\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">Must-Know</span>\n<ul>\n<li><b>&lt; 4</b> valence electrons → good <b>conductor</b>; <b>&gt; 4</b> → <b>insulator</b>.</li>\n<li>Like charges <b>repel</b>; unlike charges <b>attract</b>.</li>\n<li><b>Voltage</b> = push (volts); <b>current</b> = flow of electrons (amps).</li>\n<li>1 amp = 1 coulomb/second = 6.28 × 10¹⁸ electrons/second.</li>\n</ul></div>\n<p class=\"cite\">Source: FAA-H-8083-30B, Chapter 12 (Matter, atomic structure, static electricity, current).</p>\n", "quiz": [{"q": "The conventional current theory, advanced by Benjamin Franklin, holds that current flows:", "options": ["From positive (+) to negative (-)", "In both directions at once", "Only in AC circuits", "From negative to positive"], "correct": 0, "short": "Positive to negative", "expl": "Conventional current theory, advanced by Franklin, visualizes current as flowing from positive (+) to negative (-). Electron flow is the opposite.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-14", "topic": "Conventional vs Electron Flow"}, {"q": "On an irregularly-shaped charged object, the greatest charge concentrates at:", "options": ["The center", "The areas of least curvature", "The flat surfaces", "The points or areas of sharpest curvature"], "correct": 3, "short": "The points / sharpest curvature", "expl": "The charge on an irregularly-shaped object is not evenly distributed; the greatest charge is at the points, or areas of sharpest curvature.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-5", "topic": "Static Electricity"}, {"q": "An atom is said to have a NEUTRAL charge when:", "options": ["It has more protons than electrons", "It contains only neutrons", "It has more electrons than protons", "The positive proton charge equals the negative electron charge"], "correct": 3, "short": "Proton (+) charge = electron (-) charge", "expl": "When the total positive charge of the protons in the nucleus equals the total negative charge of the orbiting electrons, the atom has a neutral charge.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-2", "topic": "Matter & Atoms"}, {"q": "The maximum number of electrons that any shell can hold is defined by the equation:", "options": ["n^2", "n/2", "2n", "2n^2"], "correct": 3, "short": "2n^2", "expl": "Electron capacity = 2n^2, where n is the energy level. The first shell holds 2 electrons, the second 8, the third 18, and so on.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-2", "topic": "Matter & Atoms"}, {"q": "Per the fundamental law of static electricity (and magnetism), how do charges behave?", "options": ["All charges attract", "All charges repel", "Like charges attract; unlike charges repel", "Like charges repel; unlike charges attract"], "correct": 3, "short": "Like charges repel; unlike charges attract", "expl": "One of the most fundamental laws of static electricity and magnetism: like charges repel each other and unlike charges attract each other.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-4", "topic": "Static Electricity"}, {"q": "An atom that has LOST an electron becomes a positively charged particle called a:", "options": ["Free electron", "Anion", "Neutron", "Positive ion (cation)"], "correct": 3, "short": "Positive ion (cation)", "expl": "Dislodging an electron leaves the atom net positive — a positive ion or cation. An atom that GAINS an electron becomes a negative ion or anion.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-2", "topic": "Matter & Atoms"}, {"q": "How does the FAA officially define the direction of current flow?", "options": ["Electron flow (negative to positive)", "Conventional flow (positive to negative)", "Bidirectional flow", "Drift flow"], "correct": 0, "short": "Electron flow (negative to positive)", "expl": "The FAA officially defines current flow using electron theory: negative to positive. Electron flow is what actually happens — electrons flow from the negative source to the positive.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-14", "topic": "Conventional vs Electron Flow"}, {"q": "One ampere (A) of current is equivalent to how much charge passing a point in one second?", "options": ["1 joule", "1 ohm", "1 volt", "1 coulomb"], "correct": 3, "short": "1 coulomb", "expl": "1 A = 1 coulomb per second. One ampere equals one coulomb of charge passing through a conductor in one second.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-17", "topic": "EMF, Current & Units"}, {"q": "The relatively slow average velocity at which electrons drift toward the positive end of a conductor is called the:", "options": ["Drift velocity", "Phase velocity", "Escape velocity", "Saturation velocity"], "correct": 0, "short": "Drift velocity", "expl": "The average velocity of the charge is called drift velocity — a relatively slow speed — even though the effect of current is nearly instantaneous.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-17", "topic": "EMF, Current & Units"}]}, {"id": "b4m1s2", "title": "Conductors, wire & sources of electricity", "lesson": "\n<p><b>The Big Idea.</b> A <b>conductor</b> lets electrons move easily; an\n<b>insulator</b> fights their movement. The best everyday conductors, in order,\nare <b>silver, copper, gold, aluminum</b>. Copper wins for wiring because it's a\ngreat conductor and much cheaper than silver or gold. <b>Aluminum</b> is used\nwhere <b>weight matters</b> (it's about half the weight of copper for the same job)\n— common in large aircraft feeder cables.</p>\n<h3>Wire size — the backwards number</h3>\n<p>Aircraft wire is sized by the <b>American Wire Gauge (AWG)</b>. Here's the trap:\n<b>the bigger the gauge number, the SMALLER the wire.</b> A 4-gauge wire is fat;\na 20-gauge wire is thin. Think of it like shotgun gauges — small number, big bore.</p>\n<h3>Where electricity comes from</h3>\n<p>There are <b>six</b> common sources of electricity (EMF): <b>friction</b> (static),\n<b>pressure</b> (piezoelectric crystals), <b>heat</b> (thermocouples), <b>light</b>\n(photocells), <b>chemical</b> (batteries), and <b>magnetism</b> (generators). For\naircraft, the two big ones are <b>chemical</b> (the battery) and <b>magnetism</b>\n(the generator/alternator).</p>\n<h3>Metric prefixes you must recognize</h3>\n<p><b>kilo (k)</b> = ×1,000; <b>mega (M)</b> = ×1,000,000; <b>milli (m)</b> = ÷1,000;\n<b>micro (µ)</b> = ÷1,000,000. So 2 kΩ = 2,000 ohms; 10 mA = 0.010 amp.</p>\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">Must-Know</span>\n<ul>\n<li>Best conductors, best→worst: <b>silver → copper → gold → aluminum</b>.</li>\n<li>AWG: <b>bigger number = smaller wire.</b></li>\n<li>Six EMF sources: friction, pressure, heat, light, <b>chemical, magnetism</b>.</li>\n<li>k = ×1000, M = ×1,000,000, m = ÷1000, µ = ÷1,000,000.</li>\n</ul></div>\n<p class=\"cite\">Source: FAA-H-8083-30B, Chapter 12 (Conductors/insulators, wire gauge, sources of EMF, units).</p>\n", "quiz": [{"q": "Producing a voltage by applying mechanical pressure to a crystal such as quartz is called the:", "options": ["Seebeck effect", "Hall effect", "Piezoelectric effect", "Photovoltaic effect"], "correct": 2, "short": "Piezoelectric effect", "expl": "Piezoelectric generation results from mechanical pressure on a dielectric crystal (e.g., quartz, Rochelle salt). The crystal produces a voltage proportional to the stress.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-22", "topic": "Sources of Electricity"}, {"q": "In wire measurement standards used in North America, one 'mil' equals:", "options": ["One inch", "One-thousandth of an inch", "One-tenth of an inch", "One-hundredth of an inch"], "correct": 1, "short": "One-thousandth of an inch", "expl": "The mil (one-thousandth of an inch) is the unit of diameter; the foot is the unit length. A wire of 1 mil diameter and 1 foot length is the unit size (mil-foot).", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-20", "topic": "Conductors & Wire"}, {"q": "The amount of resistance increase of a 1-ohm sample per degree rise in temperature above 0 degrees C is called the:", "options": ["Permeability", "Temperature coefficient of resistance", "Reluctance", "Resistivity"], "correct": 1, "short": "Temperature coefficient of resistance", "expl": "The temperature coefficient of resistance is the increase in resistance of a 1-ohm sample per degree rise above 0 degrees C. For copper it is approximately 0.00427 ohm.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-19", "topic": "Conductors & Wire"}, {"q": "A device that converts light energy directly into electricity is a:", "options": ["Thermocouple", "Solar (photovoltaic) cell", "Piezo crystal", "Rectifier"], "correct": 1, "short": "Solar (photovoltaic) cell", "expl": "A solar cell or photovoltaic cell converts light energy into electricity; photons absorbed by semiconducting material (silicon) break electrons loose to produce current.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-23", "topic": "Sources of Electricity"}, {"q": "A thermocouple generates a voltage proportional to temperature at the junction of two dissimilar metals. This thermal-electric phenomenon is the:", "options": ["Edison effect", "Photovoltaic effect", "Seebeck effect", "Piezoelectric effect"], "correct": 2, "short": "Seebeck effect", "expl": "Thermocouples generate a voltage proportional to junction temperature — the Seebeck effect. They are used to measure cylinder head temperature and Turbine Inlet Temperature (TIT).", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-23", "topic": "Sources of Electricity"}, {"q": "For most materials used as conductors, what happens to resistance as temperature rises?", "options": ["Resistance stays exactly constant", "Resistance drops to zero", "Resistance decreases", "Resistance increases"], "correct": 3, "short": "Resistance increases", "expl": "Most materials used as conductors increase in resistance as temperature increases. Carbon is an exception (it decreases); constantan and Manganin change very little.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-19", "topic": "Conductors & Wire"}, {"q": "A change of TEN wire gauge numbers represents approximately what change in cross-sectional area?", "options": ["100:1", "10:1", "5:1", "2:1"], "correct": 1, "short": "10:1", "expl": "A change of ten wire gauge numbers represents a 10:1 change in cross-sectional area.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-20", "topic": "Conductors & Wire"}, {"q": "In electrical work, the prefix 'kilo' (k) means:", "options": ["One-thousandth", "One million", "One thousand", "One-millionth"], "correct": 2, "short": "One thousand", "expl": "Kilo (k) means one thousand. Mega (M) = one million; milli (m) = one-thousandth; micro (mu) = one-millionth.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-4", "topic": "Metric Prefixes"}, {"q": "The prefix 'micro' used in electrical calculations means:", "options": ["One million", "One thousand", "One-thousandth", "One-millionth"], "correct": 3, "short": "One-millionth", "expl": "Micro (mu) means one-millionth (1/1,000,000). Milli means one-thousandth. These appear constantly with farads (microfarad) and amps.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-4", "topic": "Metric Prefixes"}]}, {"id": "b4m1s3", "title": "Ohm's Law & Watt's Law (power)", "lesson": "\n<p><b>The Big Idea.</b> <b>Ohm's Law</b> is the single most important equation in\nelectricity. It ties together the three basic quantities:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>E</b> = voltage (volts) — the push</li>\n<li><b>I</b> = current (amps) — the flow</li>\n<li><b>R</b> = resistance (ohms, Ω) — the \"friction\" that resists flow</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>The formula:</b> <code>E = I × R</code>. Rearranged: <code>I = E ÷ R</code>\nand <code>R = E ÷ I</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"box ex\"><span class=\"bt\">Example</span>\nA 28-volt system pushes current through a 14-ohm heater.\nCurrent = E ÷ R = 28 ÷ 14 = <b>2 amps</b>.</div>\n<h3>Watt's Law — power</h3>\n<p><b>Power</b> is how fast electrical energy is used, measured in <b>watts (P)</b>.\n<code>P = E × I</code> (volts × amps). Combined with Ohm's Law you also get\n<code>P = I² × R</code> and <code>P = E² ÷ R</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"box ex\"><span class=\"bt\">Example</span>\nThat heater: P = E × I = 28 × 2 = <b>56 watts</b>.</div>\n<h3>The memory tools</h3>\n<p>Use the <b>Ohm's Law \"magic triangle\"</b> (E on top, I and R on the bottom):\ncover the one you want and the other two show the math. There's a matching\n<b>power wheel</b> for the four power/voltage/current/resistance relationships.</p>\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">Must-Know</span>\n<ul>\n<li><b>E = I × R</b> · I = E/R · R = E/I</li>\n<li><b>P = E × I</b> · P = I²R · P = E²/R</li>\n<li>Volts = push, Amps = flow, Ohms = resistance, Watts = power.</li>\n</ul></div>\n<p class=\"cite\">Source: FAA-H-8083-30B, Chapter 12 (Ohm's Law, Watt's Law / power).</p>\n", "quiz": [{"q": "Ohm's Law states that current equals voltage divided by resistance. Which equation expresses this?", "options": ["I = E x R", "I = R / E", "I = E / R", "E = I / R"], "correct": 2, "short": "I = E / R", "expl": "Ohm's Law: I = E/R, where I is current (A), E is voltage (V), and R is resistance (ohms). Transposed: E = I x R and R = E/I.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-17", "topic": "Ohm's Law"}, {"q": "Using the third form of the power equation, P = E^2 / R, what is the power for 24 volts across 20 ohms?", "options": ["1.2 watts", "480 watts", "28.8 watts", "120 watts"], "correct": 2, "short": "28.8 watts", "expl": "P = E^2/R = (24 V)^2 / 20 ohms = 576 / 20 = 28.8 W. This matches the handbook's worked example.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-22", "topic": "Power & Watt's Law"}, {"q": "Transposing Ohm's Law to solve for resistance gives which equation?", "options": ["R = E / I", "R = E + I", "R = I x E", "R = I / E"], "correct": 0, "short": "R = E / I", "expl": "Dividing E = I(R) by I gives the third form of Ohm's Law: R = E/I.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-18", "topic": "Ohm's Law"}, {"q": "One horsepower is defined as the power required to move how much weight one foot in one minute?", "options": ["1,000 pounds", "746 pounds", "33,000 pounds", "550 pounds"], "correct": 2, "short": "33,000 pounds", "expl": "One horsepower is required to move 33,000 pounds 1 foot in 1 minute, which works out to 550 ft-lb/s.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-21", "topic": "Power & Watt's Law"}, {"q": "Using the voltage divider formula EX = (RX/RT) x ES with RT = 1,000 ohms, ES = 100 V, the drop across a 300-ohm resistor is:", "options": ["30 V", "100 V", "10 V", "60 V"], "correct": 0, "short": "30 volts", "expl": "EX = (300/1000) x 100 = 30 V. The voltage drop across a resistor equals the ratio of its resistance to total resistance, times the source voltage.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-39", "topic": "Voltage Dividers"}, {"q": "Transposing Ohm's Law to solve for voltage gives which equation?", "options": ["E = I / R", "E = I + R", "E = R / I", "E = I x R"], "correct": 3, "short": "E = I x R", "expl": "Multiplying I = E/R by R gives the second form of Ohm's Law: E = I(R).", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-18", "topic": "Ohm's Law"}, {"q": "One horsepower of mechanical power is equivalent to how many watts of electrical power?", "options": ["33,000 watts", "1,000 watts", "746 watts", "550 watts"], "correct": 2, "short": "746 watts", "expl": "An electric motor rated at 1 horsepower requires 746 watts of electrical energy. (33,000 ft-lb/min and 550 ft-lb/s describe the mechanical definition of horsepower.)", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-21", "topic": "Power & Watt's Law"}, {"q": "Using the handbook's graph example with a constant 20-ohm resistance, if 60 volts are applied the current is:", "options": ["0.5 A", "3 A", "6 A", "30 A"], "correct": 1, "short": "3 amperes", "expl": "I = E/R = 60 V / 20 ohms = 3 A. This matches the handbook's linear-relationship graph (E=10 gives 0.5 A, E=60 gives 3 A, E=120 gives 6 A).", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-17", "topic": "Ohm's Law"}, {"q": "If 24 volts produces 3 amperes of current, what is the resistance?", "options": ["27 ohms", "8 ohms", "72 ohms", "0.125 ohm"], "correct": 1, "short": "8 ohms", "expl": "R = E/I = 24 V / 3 A = 8 ohms.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-18", "topic": "Ohm's Law"}]}, {"id": "b4m1s4", "title": "Resistors & the color code", "lesson": "\n<p><b>The Big Idea.</b> A <b>resistor</b> is a part that deliberately adds\nresistance to control current or drop voltage. Small resistors are marked with\n<b>colored bands</b> instead of numbers, so you have to read the color code.</p>\n<h3>The resistor color code</h3>\n<p>Memorize the colors 0–9 in order. A classic (cleaned-up) mnemonic is:\n<b>B</b>lack <b>B</b>rown <b>R</b>ed <b>O</b>range <b>Y</b>ellow <b>G</b>reen\n<b>B</b>lue <b>V</b>iolet <b>G</b>rey <b>W</b>hite.</p>\n<table><tr><th>Color</th><th>Digit</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Black</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>Brown</td><td>1</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Red</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>Orange</td><td>3</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Yellow</td><td>4</td></tr><tr><td>Green</td><td>5</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Blue</td><td>6</td></tr><tr><td>Violet</td><td>7</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Grey</td><td>8</td></tr><tr><td>White</td><td>9</td></tr></table>\n<p><b>How to read it (4-band):</b> Band 1 = first digit, Band 2 = second digit,\nBand 3 = <b>multiplier</b> (number of zeros to add), Band 4 = <b>tolerance</b>\n(gold = ±5%, silver = ±10%, none = ±20%).</p>\n<div class=\"box ex\"><span class=\"bt\">Example</span>\nBrown-Black-Red = 1, 0, then ×100 → <b>1,000 Ω (1 kΩ)</b>. A gold 4th band = ±5%.</div>\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">Must-Know</span>\n<ul>\n<li>Color digits: Black 0, Brown 1, Red 2, Orange 3, Yellow 4, Green 5, Blue 6, Violet 7, Grey 8, White 9.</li>\n<li>Band 3 = multiplier (zeros); Band 4 = tolerance.</li>\n<li>Tolerance: <b>gold ±5%, silver ±10%, no band ±20%.</b></li>\n</ul></div>\n<p class=\"cite\">Source: FAA-H-8083-30B, Chapter 12 (Resistors and the color code).</p>\n", "quiz": [{"q": "On a resistor, a GOLD fourth (tolerance) band indicates what tolerance?", "options": ["10 percent", "1 percent", "20 percent", "5 percent"], "correct": 3, "short": "5 percent", "expl": "A gold fourth band = 5 percent tolerance; silver = 10 percent; no fourth band = 20 percent.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-25", "topic": "Resistors & Color Code"}, {"q": "The general purpose of a resistor in a circuit is to:", "options": ["Oppose AC only", "Increase voltage", "Store charge", "Limit the amount of current flow"], "correct": 3, "short": "Limit the amount of current flow", "expl": "The general use of a resistor is to limit the amount of current flow. Heat is typically the unwanted loss of energy (except in heating elements).", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-24", "topic": "Resistors & Color Code"}, {"q": "A potentiometer is considered a THREE-terminal device and is used to vary the:", "options": ["Frequency", "Voltage in a circuit", "Current in a circuit", "Resistance of the source"], "correct": 1, "short": "Voltage in a circuit", "expl": "The potentiometer is a three-terminal device (terminal 3 is the wiper). While a rheostat varies current, the potentiometer is used to vary the voltage in a circuit.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-27", "topic": "Resistors & Color Code"}, {"q": "The percentage variation allowed between a resistor's marked value and its actual value is known as its:", "options": ["Wattage", "Tolerance", "Resistivity", "Reactance"], "correct": 1, "short": "Tolerance", "expl": "Tolerance is the allowable percentage variation between marked and actual value. A 5 percent resistor is not more than 5 percent higher or lower than the marked value.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-24", "topic": "Resistors & Color Code"}, {"q": "A resistor reads red, green, yellow. Its resistance value is:", "options": ["250,000 ohms", "25 ohms", "2,540 ohms", "2,500 ohms"], "correct": 0, "short": "250,000 ohms", "expl": "Red=2, Green=5, Yellow=4 (number of zeros). So 25 followed by four zeros = 250,000 ohms. With no fourth band, tolerance is 20 percent (50,000 ohms).", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-25", "topic": "Resistors & Color Code"}, {"q": "Which variable resistor is used to VARY the current in a circuit and is connected in series?", "options": ["Rheostat", "Photocell", "Potentiometer", "Thermistor"], "correct": 0, "short": "Rheostat", "expl": "A rheostat is a variable resistor used to vary current; connected in series, increasing its resistance increases total resistance and decreases current.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-26", "topic": "Resistors & Color Code"}, {"q": "On the resistor color code, what numerical value does the color RED represent?", "options": ["2", "0", "5", "1"], "correct": 0, "short": "2", "expl": "Color code values: Black 0, Brown 1, Red 2, Orange 3, Yellow 4, Green 5, Blue 6, Violet 7, Gray 8, White 9.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-25", "topic": "Resistors & Color Code"}, {"q": "On an end-to-center band resistor, the THIRD color band normally indicates:", "options": ["The first digit", "The second digit", "The number of zeros to add to the first two digits", "The wattage rating"], "correct": 2, "short": "Number of zeros to add", "expl": "The third band indicates the number of zeros to add to the two digits from the first and second bands (except gold = x 0.10, silver = x 0.01).", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-25", "topic": "Resistors & Color Code"}, {"q": "On a resistor, a SILVER fourth (tolerance) band indicates what tolerance?", "options": ["20 percent", "1 percent", "5 percent", "10 percent"], "correct": 3, "short": "10 percent", "expl": "A silver fourth band = 10 percent tolerance. Gold = 5 percent; no band = 20 percent.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-25", "topic": "Resistors & Color Code"}]}]}, {"n": 2, "day": "Unit 2", "title": "DC Circuits", "ref": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.12 · Series/parallel, faults, meters", "sections": [{"id": "b4m2s1", "title": "Series & parallel circuits", "lesson": "\n<p><b>The Big Idea.</b> Circuits are wired two basic ways, and the rules are\nmirror-images of each other.</p>\n<h3>Series circuit — one path</h3>\n<p>Parts are in a single line, like beads on one string. The same current flows\nthrough every part. <b>Resistances ADD up</b> (R_total = R1 + R2 + R3). The\n<b>voltage divides</b> across the parts. <b>Trap:</b> if one part opens (a bulb\nburns out), the whole circuit dies — like old Christmas lights.</p>\n<h3>Parallel circuit — multiple paths</h3>\n<p>Parts sit side-by-side, each with its own path. The <b>voltage is the same</b>\nacross every branch. The <b>current divides</b> among the branches. Total\nresistance is <b>LESS than the smallest branch</b> resistor (more paths = easier\nflow). If one branch opens, the others keep working — that's why aircraft (and\nyour house) wire loads in parallel.</p>\n<table><tr><th></th><th>Series</th><th>Parallel</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Current</td><td>Same everywhere</td><td>Divides</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Voltage</td><td>Divides</td><td>Same everywhere</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Total R</td><td>Adds up</td><td>Less than smallest</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">Must-Know</span>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Series:</b> current same, voltage divides, resistances ADD.</li>\n<li><b>Parallel:</b> voltage same, current divides, total R &lt; smallest resistor.</li>\n<li>Series open = whole circuit dead; parallel open = other branches still work.</li>\n</ul></div>\n<p class=\"cite\">Source: FAA-H-8083-30B, Chapter 12 (Series and parallel DC circuits).</p>\n", "quiz": [{"q": "Three resistors of 10, 20, and 30 ohms are connected in SERIES. What is the total resistance?", "options": ["6 ohms", "20 ohms", "60 ohms", "30 ohms"], "correct": 2, "short": "60 ohms", "expl": "Series total resistance is the simple sum: 10 + 20 + 30 = 60 ohms.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-34", "topic": "Series & Parallel Circuits"}, {"q": "In a parallel circuit acting as a current divider, branches with higher resistance carry:", "options": ["More current", "Less current", "No current", "The same current as low-resistance branches"], "correct": 1, "short": "Less current", "expl": "Branch currents are inversely proportional to the ohmic values: branches with higher resistance have less current than those with lower resistance.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-41", "topic": "Series & Parallel Circuits"}, {"q": "In a PARALLEL circuit, the total resistance is always:", "options": ["Equal to the largest branch", "The average of the branches", "Less than the value of the smallest resistor", "Equal to the sum of the branches"], "correct": 2, "short": "Less than the smallest branch", "expl": "The total resistance of a parallel combination is always less than the value of the smallest resistor, because adding branches gives current more paths.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-39", "topic": "Series & Parallel Circuits"}, {"q": "Using the product-over-sum formula, a 500-ohm and 400-ohm resistor in parallel give a total resistance of approximately:", "options": ["900 ohms", "450 ohms", "100 ohms", "222.22 ohms"], "correct": 3, "short": "222.22 ohms", "expl": "RT = (500 x 400) / (500 + 400) = 200,000 / 900 = 222.22 ohms. This is the handbook's worked example.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-41", "topic": "Series & Parallel Circuits"}, {"q": "A circuit that is a combination of resistors in series and in parallel is called a:", "options": ["Series-parallel circuit", "Tank circuit", "Resonant circuit", "Bridge circuit"], "correct": 0, "short": "Series-parallel circuit", "expl": "Most real circuits are series-parallel — combinations of resistors in parallel and in series; the same series and parallel rules apply.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-42", "topic": "Series & Parallel Circuits"}, {"q": "In the handbook example, a current of 1 mA flows through R1=1k, R2=3k, R3=5k in series. The source voltage equals:", "options": ["3 volts", "1 volt", "5 volts", "9 volts"], "correct": 3, "short": "9 volts", "expl": "Voltage drops: 1 V + 3 V + 5 V = 9 V. Or ES = I x RT = 1 mA x 9 k = 9 V. The sum of the drops equals the source voltage.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-36", "topic": "Series & Parallel Circuits"}, {"q": "In the handbook's series-parallel example (R1 = 1k in series with R2=2k parallel R3=3k, ES = 24 V), the total resistance RT is:", "options": ["6 k-ohm", "5 k-ohm", "1.2 k-ohm", "2.2 k-ohm"], "correct": 3, "short": "2.2 k-ohms", "expl": "R23 = (2k x 3k)/(2k+3k) = 6,000k/5k = 1.2k. RT = R1 + R23 = 1k + 1.2k = 2.2k ohms.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-43", "topic": "Series & Parallel Circuits"}, {"q": "In that same series-parallel example (RT = 2.2 k-ohm, ES = 24 V), the total current IT is approximately:", "options": ["10.9 mA", "24 mA", "52.8 mA", "2.2 mA"], "correct": 0, "short": "10.9 mA", "expl": "IT = ES/RT = 24 V / 2.2k = 10.9 mA (handbook worked example).", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-43", "topic": "Series & Parallel Circuits"}, {"q": "In a SERIES circuit, the total resistance equals:", "options": ["The sum of the individual resistors", "Less than the smallest resistor", "The product of the resistors", "The smallest resistor value"], "correct": 0, "short": "The sum of the resistors", "expl": "For resistors in series, total resistance = R1 + R2 + R3 + ... + RN (a simple sum).", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-34", "topic": "Series & Parallel Circuits"}]}, {"id": "b4m2s2", "title": "Kirchhoff's laws & voltage dividers", "lesson": "\n<p><b>The Big Idea.</b> <b>Kirchhoff's two laws</b> are the bookkeeping rules that\nmake circuit math balance — nothing is created or lost.</p>\n<h3>Kirchhoff's Current Law (KCL)</h3>\n<p>At any junction (node), <b>the current going IN equals the current going OUT.</b>\nElectrons don't pile up — whatever flows into a point flows back out.</p>\n<h3>Kirchhoff's Voltage Law (KVL)</h3>\n<p>Around any complete loop, <b>the voltage drops add up to the source voltage.</b>\nEvery volt the source pushes gets \"spent\" across the parts in the loop.</p>\n<h3>Voltage dividers</h3>\n<p>A <b>voltage divider</b> is just a series string of resistors used on purpose to\ntap off a smaller voltage. The bigger the resistor, the bigger its share of the\nvoltage drop (drop is proportional to resistance).</p>\n<div class=\"box ex\"><span class=\"bt\">Example</span>\nTwo equal resistors in series across 28 V each drop <b>14 V</b> — half the total\neach, because they share the voltage equally.</div>\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">Must-Know</span>\n<ul>\n<li><b>KCL:</b> current IN = current OUT at any junction.</li>\n<li><b>KVL:</b> sum of voltage drops around a loop = source voltage.</li>\n<li>In a series divider, voltage drop is <b>proportional to resistance</b>.</li>\n</ul></div>\n<p class=\"cite\">Source: FAA-H-8083-30B, Chapter 12 (Kirchhoff's laws, voltage dividers).</p>\n", "quiz": [{"q": "Using Kirchhoff's Current Law: if branch currents I1 = 5 mA and I2 = 12 mA enter a node, the total current is:", "options": ["7 mA", "12 mA", "60 mA", "17 mA"], "correct": 3, "short": "17 mA", "expl": "IT = I1 + I2 = 5 mA + 12 mA = 17 mA. This is the handbook's worked example.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-41", "topic": "Kirchhoff's Laws"}, {"q": "Using the voltage divider formula EX = (RX/RT) x ES with RT = 1,000 ohms, ES = 100 V, the drop across a 300-ohm resistor is:", "options": ["30 V", "100 V", "10 V", "60 V"], "correct": 0, "short": "30 volts", "expl": "EX = (300/1000) x 100 = 30 V. The voltage drop across a resistor equals the ratio of its resistance to total resistance, times the source voltage.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-39", "topic": "Voltage Dividers"}, {"q": "Kirchhoff's Current Law states that at any junction (node):", "options": ["Current leaving exceeds current entering", "Current is the same in every branch", "Voltage drops sum to zero", "The sum of currents entering equals the sum of currents leaving"], "correct": 3, "short": "Currents in = currents out", "expl": "Kirchhoff's Current Law: the sum of the currents into a junction equals the sum of the currents flowing out of that same junction.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-41", "topic": "Kirchhoff's Laws"}, {"q": "A 24-volt source feeds three series resistors. Two drops are 12 V and 10 V. Using KVL, the third drop (E3) is:", "options": ["46 volts", "2 volts", "12 volts", "22 volts"], "correct": 1, "short": "2 volts", "expl": "24 - 12 - 10 - E3 = 0, so E3 = 2 volts. This is the handbook's worked example.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-36", "topic": "Kirchhoff's Laws"}, {"q": "In a voltage divider, the small current that flows through the divider in addition to the load currents is called the:", "options": ["Branch current", "Bleeder current", "Eddy current", "Drift current"], "correct": 1, "short": "Bleeder current", "expl": "In addition to the load currents (I1, I2, I3), some bleeder current (IB) flows. The total current IT is the sum of all currents.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-37", "topic": "Voltage Dividers"}, {"q": "Total current into a node is 75 mA. Two of three branches carry 30 mA and 20 mA. The third branch carries:", "options": ["125 mA", "50 mA", "15 mA", "25 mA"], "correct": 3, "short": "25 mA", "expl": "IT = I1 + I2 + I3, so 75 = 30 + I2 + 20, giving I2 = 25 mA. This is the handbook's worked example.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-41", "topic": "Kirchhoff's Laws"}, {"q": "A device that makes it possible to obtain more than one voltage from a single power source — usually resistors connected in series with fixed/movable contacts — is a:", "options": ["Voltage divider", "Rectifier", "Transformer", "Current divider"], "correct": 0, "short": "Voltage divider", "expl": "Voltage dividers make it possible to obtain more than one voltage from a single power source; they consist of resistors in series with tap contacts.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-37", "topic": "Voltage Dividers"}, {"q": "Stated another way, Kirchhoff's Voltage Law tells us the source voltage equals:", "options": ["The sum of the voltage drops", "Zero", "The total current", "The largest single voltage drop"], "correct": 0, "short": "Sum of the voltage drops", "expl": "The source voltage equals the sum of the voltage drops: ES = E1 + E2 + E3 + ... + EN.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-36", "topic": "Kirchhoff's Laws"}, {"q": "Kirchhoff's Voltage Law states that around any closed loop, the algebraic sum of all voltages is:", "options": ["Equal to the current", "Equal to the largest drop", "Zero", "Equal to total resistance"], "correct": 2, "short": "Zero", "expl": "Kirchhoff's Voltage Law: the algebraic sum of all voltages around a closed loop is zero. Equivalently, the sum of all voltage drops equals the total source voltage.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-36", "topic": "Kirchhoff's Laws"}]}, {"id": "b4m2s3", "title": "Open & short circuits, protection & switches", "lesson": "\n<p><b>The Big Idea.</b> Circuits fail two ways, and each has an opposite symptom —\nplus protective devices that save the wiring when a fault happens.</p>\n<h3>Open vs. short</h3>\n<p>An <b>OPEN</b> circuit is a break in the path — like a cut wire or blown fuse.\n<b>No current flows</b> (the load goes dead). A <b>SHORT</b> circuit is an\nunwanted low-resistance path (usually to ground). Current shoots up dangerously\nhigh because resistance drops near zero — that's what starts fires and pops\nbreakers.</p>\n<h3>Circuit protection</h3>\n<p><b>Fuses</b> and <b>circuit breakers</b> protect the <b>WIRE, not the device</b>.\nA fuse is a thin metal link that melts and must be replaced. A circuit breaker\ntrips and can be <b>reset</b>. Aircraft breakers are the <b>\"trip-free\"</b> type:\nyou can't hold them closed against a fault. <b>Rule: reset a tripped breaker\nonly ONCE.</b> If it trips again, leave it — there's a real fault.</p>\n<h3>Switches &amp; relays</h3>\n<p>A <b>switch</b> makes or breaks a circuit by hand. A <b>relay</b> (or\n<b>solenoid</b>) is an electrically-operated switch: a small control current\nenergizes a coil that throws heavy contacts — letting a tiny cockpit switch\ncontrol a big load like the starter.</p>\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">Must-Know</span>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Open</b> = broken path, no current; <b>Short</b> = low-R path, current too high.</li>\n<li>Fuses/breakers protect the <b>WIRE</b>. Reset a breaker <b>once</b> only.</li>\n<li>A <b>relay</b> = an electrically-controlled switch (small current switches a big load).</li>\n</ul></div>\n<p class=\"cite\">Source: FAA-H-8083-30B, Chapter 12 (Open/short circuits, circuit protection, switches & relays).</p>\n", "quiz": [{"q": "In most fuses, the metal strip that melts to open the circuit is made of an alloy of:", "options": ["Copper and aluminum", "Iron and nickel", "Lead and silver", "Tin and bismuth"], "correct": 3, "short": "Tin and bismuth", "expl": "In most fuses, the metal strip is made of an alloy of tin and bismuth, which melts and opens the circuit when current exceeds the rated capacity.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-28", "topic": "Circuit Protection"}, {"q": "A switch that stays open except when held in the closed position is identified as:", "options": ["Locking", "Normally closed (NC)", "Double throw", "Normally open (NO)"], "correct": 3, "short": "Normally open (NO)", "expl": "A switch that stays open except when held in the closed position is a normally open (NO) switch. One that stays closed except when held open is normally closed (NC).", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-31", "topic": "Switches & Relays"}, {"q": "A switch that allows a connection between two contacts — either open in one position or closed in the other — is designated:", "options": ["DPDT", "SPDT", "DPST", "SPST"], "correct": 3, "short": "SPST (single-pole, single-throw)", "expl": "The single-pole, single-throw (SPST) switch allows a connection between two contacts; the circuit is open in one position and closed in the other.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-30", "topic": "Switches & Relays"}, {"q": "A circuit-protection device similar to a fuse but made of copper, used in heavy-current circuits (30 amp or greater) and which must be replaced once opened, is a:", "options": ["Current limiter", "Relay", "Circuit breaker", "Thermal switch"], "correct": 0, "short": "Current limiter", "expl": "The current limiter's link is usually copper and stands a considerable overload briefly; it opens in over-current heavy circuits (30 A+), is used to sectionalize a bus, and must be replaced.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-29", "topic": "Circuit Protection"}, {"q": "A circuit that is not a complete or continuous path — representing an infinitely large resistance so no current flows — is a(n):", "options": ["Parallel circuit", "Short circuit", "Closed circuit", "Open circuit"], "correct": 3, "short": "Open circuit", "expl": "An open circuit is not a complete or continuous path; it represents an infinitely large resistance. Switches and burned-out components are common causes.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-79", "topic": "Open & Short Circuits"}, {"q": "The state of being continuous, uninterrupted, or connected together (a circuit that is not broken) is called:", "options": ["Reactance", "Discontinuity", "Saturation", "Continuity"], "correct": 3, "short": "Continuity", "expl": "Continuity is the state of being continuous, uninterrupted, or connected together — the opposite of a broken or open circuit.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-79", "topic": "Open & Short Circuits"}, {"q": "A switch that makes a connection from one set of contacts to either of two other sets of contacts is designated:", "options": ["SPST", "SPDT", "DPST", "DPDT"], "correct": 3, "short": "DPDT (double-pole, double-throw)", "expl": "The double-pole, double-throw (DPDT) switch makes a connection from one set of contacts to either of two other sets of contacts.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-30", "topic": "Switches & Relays"}, {"q": "When checking for an open in a PARALLEL circuit, why can a voltmeter NOT be used effectively?", "options": ["It cannot measure AC", "Across an open resistor it reads the voltage drop of the parallel branch", "It draws too much current", "It has too high resistance"], "correct": 1, "short": "Reads parallel branch voltage", "expl": "A voltmeter cannot be used in a parallel circuit for an open, since placed across an open resistor it reads the voltage drop in a parallel branch. Use an ammeter or modified ohmmeter.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-84", "topic": "Open & Short Circuits"}, {"q": "Which type of circuit breaker is NOT allowed in aircraft?", "options": ["Magnetic type", "Automatic reset type", "Thermal overload type", "Manually reset type"], "correct": 1, "short": "Automatic reset type", "expl": "Automatic reset type circuit breakers are not allowed in aircraft. Most circuit breakers must be reset by hand.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-29", "topic": "Circuit Protection"}]}, {"id": "b4m2s4", "title": "Measuring instruments (volt/amp/ohm)", "lesson": "\n<p><b>The Big Idea.</b> To troubleshoot, you measure. Three instruments cover the\nbasics, and how you connect them matters.</p>\n<h3>The three meters</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Voltmeter</b> — measures voltage. Connect it <b>in PARALLEL</b> (across) the\npart. It has <b>very high</b> internal resistance so it barely draws current.</li>\n<li><b>Ammeter</b> — measures current. Connect it <b>in SERIES</b> (in the line) so\nall the current flows through it. It has <b>very low</b> resistance.\n<b>Trap:</b> never put an ammeter across a battery — its low resistance makes a\nshort and it will be damaged.</li>\n<li><b>Ohmmeter</b> — measures resistance. Use it only on a <b>de-energized</b>\n(power-off) circuit; it supplies its own small battery.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A <b>multimeter</b> combines all three. The classic analog meter movement is the\n<b>d'Arsonval</b> (a coil that turns a needle in a magnetic field). Digital meters\nshow a number directly and have even higher input resistance.</p>\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">Must-Know</span>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Voltmeter → parallel</b>, high resistance.</li>\n<li><b>Ammeter → series</b>, low resistance (never across a source).</li>\n<li><b>Ohmmeter → power OFF</b> only; it has its own battery.</li>\n</ul></div>\n<p class=\"cite\">Source: FAA-H-8083-30B, Chapter 12 (Measuring instruments — volt/amp/ohm meters).</p>\n", "quiz": [{"q": "On an ohmmeter, when the leads are OPEN (not connected), the pointer indicates:", "options": ["Half-scale", "A short circuit", "Infinite resistance", "Zero ohms"], "correct": 2, "short": "Infinite resistance", "expl": "When the ohmmeter leads are open, the meter indicates infinite resistance (an open circuit). When the leads are shorted, it reads zero.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-71", "topic": "Measuring Instruments"}, {"q": "A high-range ohmmeter containing a hand-operated generator, used to measure insulation resistance with a high 'breakdown' voltage, is a:", "options": ["Wheatstone bridge", "Megger (megohmmeter)", "Ammeter", "Multimeter"], "correct": 1, "short": "Megger (megohmmeter)", "expl": "The megger (megohmmeter) is a high-range ohmmeter with a hand-operated generator, used to measure insulation resistance, ground, continuity, and short circuits at high potential.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-72", "topic": "Measuring Instruments"}, {"q": "The most accurate ammeter readings are obtained at approximately what point on the scale?", "options": ["Quarter-scale deflection", "Zero deflection", "Full-scale deflection", "Half-scale deflection"], "correct": 3, "short": "Half-scale deflection", "expl": "The most accurate readings are obtained at approximately half-scale deflection; start with the highest range and work down.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-70", "topic": "Measuring Instruments"}, {"q": "Compared with older analog meters, digital multimeters (DMM) and digital voltmeters (DVM) offer greater accuracy and:", "options": ["Slower readings", "Higher input resistance (less loading effect)", "Lower input resistance (more loading)", "No need for a power source"], "correct": 1, "short": "Higher input resistance", "expl": "The DVM/DMM offers greater accuracy, less ambiguity, and higher input resistance, which gives it less of a loading effect on a circuit.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-80", "topic": "Measuring Instruments"}, {"q": "Voltmeter sensitivity is defined in terms of:", "options": ["Resistance per volt (ohms/volt)", "Volts per ampere", "Amps per ohm", "Watts per volt"], "correct": 0, "short": "Resistance per volt (ohms/volt)", "expl": "Voltmeter sensitivity is defined in terms of resistance per volt (ohms/volt). A 50 microamp movement has a sensitivity of 20k ohms/volt.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-70", "topic": "Measuring Instruments"}, {"q": "Electric power is measured by means of a wattmeter, which has two elements (one for current, one for voltage) and is usually of which type?", "options": ["d'Arsonval type", "Electrodynamometer type", "Inclined coil type", "Moving iron vane type"], "correct": 1, "short": "Electrodynamometer type", "expl": "Because power is the product of current and voltage, a wattmeter needs two elements and is usually of the electrodynamometer type; it can measure AC or DC power.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-76", "topic": "Measuring Instruments"}, {"q": "The amount of current required to drive a meter movement to full-scale deflection is called the meter's:", "options": ["Current sensitivity", "Multiplier", "Resistance", "Damping"], "correct": 0, "short": "Current sensitivity", "expl": "Current sensitivity is the amount of current required to drive the movement to full-scale deflection (e.g., a 1 mA movement needs 1 mA for full scale).", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-68", "topic": "Measuring Instruments"}, {"q": "The basic DC meter movement (used in the ammeter, voltmeter, and ohmmeter) that deflects a pointer in proportion to current through a coil is the:", "options": ["Electrodynamometer movement", "Moving iron vane movement", "d'Arsonval movement", "Inclined coil movement"], "correct": 2, "short": "d'Arsonval movement", "expl": "The d'Arsonval meter movement is the basic DC current-measuring device used in the ammeter, voltmeter, and ohmmeter; the pointer deflects in proportion to coil current.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-68", "topic": "Measuring Instruments"}, {"q": "The combined analog instrument that includes the voltmeter, ammeter, and ohmmeter is commonly called a multimeter or:", "options": ["Megger", "CRT", "DIP", "VOM (volt-ohm-milliammeter)"], "correct": 3, "short": "VOM", "expl": "The combined voltmeter/ammeter/ohmmeter is called a multimeter or a VOM (volt-ohm-milliammeter). Digital versions are the DMM and DVM.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-80", "topic": "Measuring Instruments"}]}]}, {"n": 3, "day": "Unit 3", "title": "Magnetism, Batteries & AC Fundamentals", "ref": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.12 · Magnetism, cells, AC theory", "sections": [{"id": "b4m3s1", "title": "Magnetism & electromagnetism", "lesson": "\n<p><b>The Big Idea.</b> Magnetism and electricity are two sides of one coin. A\nmagnet has a <b>north</b> and <b>south</b> pole, and the rule mirrors static\ncharge: <b>like poles repel, unlike poles attract.</b> Invisible <b>lines of\nflux</b> run from <b>N to S</b> outside the magnet.</p>\n<h3>Electromagnetism</h3>\n<p>Here's the key discovery: <b>any current-carrying wire creates a magnetic field\naround it.</b> Coil that wire into many turns and you get an <b>electromagnet</b>\nthat's strong only while current flows. Add an iron core and it's stronger still.</p>\n<p><b>Left-hand rule (for electron flow):</b> grab the coil with your left hand,\nfingers pointing the way electrons flow, and your <b>thumb points to the north\npole</b>. This is how relays, solenoids, motors, and generators all work.</p>\n<h3>Three magnetic classes</h3>\n<p>Materials are <b>ferromagnetic</b> (iron, steel, nickel, cobalt — strongly\nattracted), <b>paramagnetic</b> (weakly attracted), or <b>diamagnetic</b>\n(weakly repelled). <b>Permeability</b> = how easily a material carries flux;\n<b>reluctance</b> = its resistance to flux (the magnetic version of resistance).</p>\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">Must-Know</span>\n<ul>\n<li>Flux runs <b>N → S</b> outside the magnet; like poles repel, unlike attract.</li>\n<li>Current in a wire makes a magnetic field; a coil = an <b>electromagnet</b>.</li>\n<li><b>Ferromagnetic</b> = iron/steel/nickel/cobalt. Permeability = ease of carrying flux.</li>\n</ul></div>\n<p class=\"cite\">Source: FAA-H-8083-30B, Chapter 12 (Magnetism and electromagnetism).</p>\n", "quiz": [{"q": "A device in which a spring-loaded iron core is pulled to a center position to complete a circuit when a coil is energized is a:", "options": ["Rectifier", "Potentiometer", "Capacitor", "Solenoid (solenoid switch/relay)"], "correct": 3, "short": "Solenoid", "expl": "The solenoid (also called solenoid switch or relay) uses a spring-loaded off-center iron core that is rapidly pulled to center when the coil is energized, completing a circuit.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-14", "topic": "Electromagnetism"}, {"q": "Magnetic lines of force behave such that:", "options": ["They only exist in air", "They cross each other freely", "They never cross one another and actually repel each other", "They have no direction"], "correct": 2, "short": "They never cross and repel each other", "expl": "Lines of force are separate, individual forces; one line never crosses another — they actually repel one another and remain parallel.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-8", "topic": "Magnetism"}, {"q": "When storing a magnet, 'keeper bars' are installed across the poles to:", "options": ["Reverse the polarity", "Increase the magnet's heat", "Add electrical charge", "Provide an easy flux path and promote retention of magnetism"], "correct": 3, "short": "Provide an easy flux path / retain magnetism", "expl": "Keeper bars provide an easy path for flux lines from one pole to the other, promoting retention of the molecules' North-South alignment.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-8", "topic": "Magnetism"}, {"q": "Which three nonferrous materials are magnetic to a limited degree?", "options": ["Silver, gold, lead", "Nickel, cobalt, and gadolinium", "Brass, bronze, zinc", "Copper, aluminum, tin"], "correct": 1, "short": "Nickel, cobalt, gadolinium", "expl": "Magnetic materials include at least three nonferrous materials magnetic to a limited degree: nickel, cobalt, and gadolinium.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-6", "topic": "Magnetism"}, {"q": "Which alloy of iron, aluminum, nickel, and cobalt is considered one of the very best permanent-magnet materials?", "options": ["Manganin", "Constantan", "Alnico", "Nichrome"], "correct": 2, "short": "Alnico", "expl": "Alnico (aluminum-nickel-cobalt) is considered one of the very best permanent-magnet materials. Others include ferrites, neodymium-iron-boron, and samarium-cobalt.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-11", "topic": "Magnetism"}, {"q": "In 1820, who discovered that a compass needle is deflected near a current-carrying conductor, demonstrating a link between electricity and magnetism?", "options": ["Oersted", "Lenz", "Ohm", "Faraday"], "correct": 0, "short": "Oersted", "expl": "In 1820, Danish physicist Hans Christian Oersted discovered that a compass needle is deflected near a current-carrying conductor, linking electricity and magnetism.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-12", "topic": "Electromagnetism"}, {"q": "Iron and its alloys, which have by far the greatest permeability, are termed:", "options": ["Diamagnetic", "Nonmagnetic", "Paramagnetic", "Ferromagnetic"], "correct": 3, "short": "Ferromagnetic", "expl": "Ferromagnetic applies to iron and its alloys, which have by far the greatest permeability. Nonferrous metals with permeability greater than one (nickel, cobalt) are paramagnetic.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-9", "topic": "Magnetism"}, {"q": "When no more lines of force can be induced into a piece of magnetic material, the material is said to be:", "options": ["Polarized", "Saturated", "Neutralized", "Demagnetized"], "correct": 1, "short": "Saturated", "expl": "There is a limit to the number of lines of force a magnet can hold; when no more can be induced, the material is saturated.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-8", "topic": "Magnetism"}, {"q": "The small amount of magnetism remaining in soft iron after the magnetizing force is removed — important to generator operation — is called:", "options": ["Coercive force", "Residual magnetism", "Permeability", "Saturation"], "correct": 1, "short": "Residual magnetism", "expl": "The small amount of magnetism remaining after the force is removed is residual magnetism, of great importance in such applications as generator operation.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-11", "topic": "Magnetism"}]}, {"id": "b4m3s2", "title": "Batteries & cells", "lesson": "\n<p><b>The Big Idea.</b> A <b>battery</b> turns chemical energy into electrical\nenergy. The building block is a <b>cell</b>; a battery is two or more cells\nconnected together. Each cell has a positive plate, a negative plate, and\n<b>electrolyte</b> between them.</p>\n<h3>Series vs. parallel cells</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Cells in <b>SERIES</b> → <b>voltages add</b>, capacity (amp-hours) stays the\nsame. (Two 6-V cells in series = 12 V.)</li>\n<li>Cells in <b>PARALLEL</b> → <b>voltage stays the same</b>, capacities (amp-hours)\nadd. (Two 6-V cells in parallel = 6 V but twice the run time.)</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>The two aircraft battery types</h3>\n<p><b>Lead-acid:</b> nominal <b>2.1 V per cell</b>; check state of charge with a\n<b>hydrometer</b> (measures electrolyte <b>specific gravity</b> — high when\ncharged, low when discharged). <b>Ni-Cad (nickel-cadmium):</b> about <b>1.2–1.25 V\nper cell</b>; state of charge <b>cannot</b> be told from specific gravity;\nwatch for <b>thermal runaway</b>. <b>Capacity is rated in ampere-hours (Ah).</b></p>\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">Must-Know</span>\n<ul>\n<li>Series cells: <b>voltage adds</b>. Parallel cells: <b>amp-hours add</b>.</li>\n<li>Lead-acid ≈ <b>2.1 V/cell</b>; charge checked by <b>specific gravity (hydrometer)</b>.</li>\n<li>Ni-Cad ≈ <b>1.2 V/cell</b>; SG doesn't show charge; beware <b>thermal runaway</b>.</li>\n<li>Capacity is measured in <b>ampere-hours (Ah)</b>.</li>\n</ul></div>\n<p class=\"cite\">Source: FAA-H-8083-30B, Chapter 12 (Batteries, cells, capacity).</p>\n", "quiz": [{"q": "For a nickel-cadmium battery, water should be added to the cells only when the battery is:", "options": ["Fully charged", "Cold", "Fully discharged", "Half charged"], "correct": 0, "short": "Fully charged", "expl": "During discharge the plates absorb electrolyte; on recharge the level rises and is highest at full charge. Therefore water should be added only when the battery is fully charged.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-92", "topic": "Batteries & Cells"}, {"q": "In a lead-acid cell, the electrolyte is sulfuric acid, the positive electrode is lead peroxide (lead dioxide), and the negative electrode is:", "options": ["Lead (spongy lead)", "Carbon", "Zinc", "Copper"], "correct": 0, "short": "Lead (spongy lead)", "expl": "The lead-acid cell uses sulfuric acid electrolyte, positive plates of lead dioxide (PbO2), and negative plates of spongy lead.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-87", "topic": "Batteries & Cells"}, {"q": "The two standard methods of charging a battery are the constant current method and the:", "options": ["Constant voltage method", "Specific-gravity method", "Constant resistance method", "Trickle-pulse method"], "correct": 0, "short": "Constant voltage method", "expl": "Batteries are charged by either the constant voltage or the constant current method. In constant voltage, charging current tapers off as the battery nears full charge.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-91", "topic": "Batteries & Cells"}, {"q": "For rating airplane battery capacity, what discharge time period has been established as the basis?", "options": ["1 hour", "20 hours", "5 hours", "10 hours"], "correct": 2, "short": "5 hours", "expl": "For airplane batteries, a period of 5 hours has been established as the discharge time used in rating battery capacity.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-89", "topic": "Batteries & Cells"}, {"q": "How many lead-acid cells in series make up a typical 12-volt battery?", "options": ["3 cells", "6 cells", "12 cells", "24 cells"], "correct": 1, "short": "6 cells", "expl": "Each lead-acid cell produces approximately 2 volts; a 12-volt battery uses 6 cells in series, and a 24-volt battery uses 12 cells.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-87", "topic": "Batteries & Cells"}, {"q": "When taking a hydrometer reading, no temperature correction is necessary as long as the electrolyte temperature is between:", "options": ["70 F and 90 F", "50 F and 70 F", "32 F and 50 F", "90 F and 110 F"], "correct": 0, "short": "70 F and 90 F", "expl": "No correction is necessary when the temperature is between 70 F and 90 F. Above 90 F or below 70 F, a correction factor must be applied.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-90", "topic": "Batteries & Cells"}, {"q": "The undesirable Ni-Cad characteristic caused by constant overcharge and infrequent discharge, technically called 'voltage depression' but commonly (and erroneously) called:", "options": ["Thermal runaway", "Sulfation", "Memory effect", "Gassing"], "correct": 2, "short": "Memory effect", "expl": "Ni-Cads exhibit 'voltage depression,' commonly but erroneously called 'memory effect.' SLA batteries do not have this characteristic.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-93", "topic": "Batteries & Cells"}, {"q": "In a new, fully-charged aircraft battery, the electrolyte is approximately 30 percent acid and 70 percent water, with a specific gravity of about:", "options": ["1.270 to 1.300", "1.200", "1.150", "1.000"], "correct": 0, "short": "About 1.270 to 1.300", "expl": "A new fully-charged battery's electrolyte is ~30 percent acid/70 percent water and is about 1.300 (1.270 at 60 F). During discharge it drops below 1.300 (to ~1.150 discharged).", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-90", "topic": "Batteries & Cells"}, {"q": "One of the first aircraft to utilize lithium-ion batteries is the:", "options": ["Piper Cub", "Airbus A320", "Cessna 172", "Boeing 787"], "correct": 3, "short": "Boeing 787", "expl": "The FAA certified lithium-ion batteries for aircraft; one of the first to use them is the Boeing 787, which uses two 32V 8-cell lithium-ion batteries.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-93", "topic": "Batteries & Cells"}]}, {"id": "b4m3s3", "title": "AC theory & values (RMS/peak)", "lesson": "\n<p><b>The Big Idea.</b> <b>DC</b> (direct current) flows one direction, steady, like\nwater down a river. <b>AC</b> (alternating current) reverses direction back and\nforth many times a second, like a tide sloshing in and out. Aircraft use both:\nDC for most systems, AC for large transport aircraft and many instruments.</p>\n<h3>Reading an AC wave</h3>\n<p>AC traces a smooth <b>sine wave</b>. One complete up-and-down is a <b>cycle</b>.\n<b>Frequency</b> = cycles per second, measured in <b>hertz (Hz)</b>. Standard\naircraft AC is <b>400 Hz</b> (vs. 60 Hz in your house) because 400 Hz lets\ngenerators and transformers be smaller and lighter.</p>\n<h3>The four AC voltage values</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Peak</b> — the highest point of the wave.</li>\n<li><b>Peak-to-peak</b> — top to bottom (2 × peak).</li>\n<li><b>RMS (effective)</b> — the value that does the same work as an equal DC\nvoltage. <b>RMS = 0.707 × peak.</b> Meters read RMS by default.</li>\n<li><b>Average</b> = 0.637 × peak.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">Must-Know</span>\n<ul>\n<li>Aircraft AC frequency = <b>400 Hz</b>; frequency is in <b>hertz</b> = cycles/second.</li>\n<li><b>RMS = 0.707 × peak</b>; <b>Average = 0.637 × peak</b>; peak-to-peak = 2 × peak.</li>\n<li>RMS is the \"effective\" value — equals the DC that does the same work.</li>\n</ul></div>\n<p class=\"cite\">Source: FAA-H-8083-30B, Chapter 12 (AC theory, frequency, RMS/peak values).</p>\n", "quiz": [{"q": "The period of a sine wave and its frequency are related how?", "options": ["Period = 1 / frequency", "Period = 2 x frequency", "Period = frequency squared", "Period = frequency"], "correct": 0, "short": "Period = 1 / frequency", "expl": "Period is inversely proportional to frequency: t = 1/f, and f = 1/t. The higher the frequency, the shorter the period.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-46", "topic": "AC Theory"}, {"q": "The distance a waveform travels during one period, indicated by the Greek letter lambda, is the:", "options": ["Period", "Wavelength", "Amplitude", "Frequency"], "correct": 1, "short": "Wavelength", "expl": "Wavelength (lambda) is the distance a waveform travels during a period, measured from one point on the waveform to the corresponding point on the next.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-47", "topic": "AC Theory"}, {"q": "The three values of AC are instantaneous, peak, and:", "options": ["Apparent", "Effective (RMS)", "Average", "Reactive"], "correct": 1, "short": "Effective (RMS)", "expl": "There are three values of AC: instantaneous, peak, and effective root mean square (RMS).", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-48", "topic": "AC Values (RMS/Peak)"}, {"q": "Which two effects occur in AC circuits that do NOT occur in DC circuits?", "options": ["Resistance and power", "Polarity and frequency", "Voltage drop and current flow", "Inductive reactance and capacitive reactance"], "correct": 3, "short": "Inductive & capacitive reactance", "expl": "Because AC constantly changes direction and intensity, inductive reactance and capacitive reactance occur in AC circuits but not in DC circuits.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-44", "topic": "AC Theory"}, {"q": "The value of an induced emf depends on three factors: the strength of the magnetic field, the speed of rotation, and:", "options": ["The wire color", "The number of wires moving through the field", "The air temperature", "The wire resistance"], "correct": 1, "short": "Number of wires in the field", "expl": "Induced emf depends on (1) number of wires moving through the field, (2) strength of the magnetic field, and (3) speed of rotation.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-44", "topic": "AC Theory"}, {"q": "Household AC is 110 volts RMS. Approximately what is the PEAK voltage?", "options": ["110 volts", "78 volts", "155 volts", "220 volts"], "correct": 2, "short": "Approximately 155 volts", "expl": "Vp = 1.414 x 110 = ~155 volts. The handbook gives this exact example: the 110 V supplied to homes is only 0.707 of the ~155 V peak.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-49", "topic": "AC Values (RMS/Peak)"}, {"q": "The largest instantaneous value of a sine wave, occurring at 90 degrees (and 270 degrees in the opposite direction), is the:", "options": ["Peak value", "Average value", "Instantaneous value", "RMS value"], "correct": 0, "short": "Peak value", "expl": "The peak value is the largest instantaneous value; the largest positive value occurs at 90 degrees and the largest negative at 270 degrees.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-48", "topic": "AC Values (RMS/Peak)"}, {"q": "Using the generator LEFT-hand rule, the SECOND finger of the left hand points in the direction of the:", "options": ["Movement of the conductor", "Magnetic lines of force", "Induced emf", "Current in the field coil"], "correct": 2, "short": "Induced emf", "expl": "Generator left-hand rule: first finger = magnetic lines of force (N to S), thumb = movement of conductor, second finger = direction of induced emf.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-44", "topic": "AC Theory"}, {"q": "How many electrical degrees are there in one complete cycle of an AC sine wave?", "options": ["360 degrees", "270 degrees", "90 degrees", "180 degrees"], "correct": 0, "short": "360 degrees", "expl": "One full sine-wave cycle spans 360 degrees: zero at 0, max at 90, zero at 180, max (opposite) at 270, zero at 360.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-49", "topic": "AC Theory"}]}]}, {"n": 4, "day": "Unit 4", "title": "AC Components & Electronics", "ref": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.12 · Reactance, machines, semiconductors", "sections": [{"id": "b4m4s1", "title": "Capacitance & inductance", "lesson": "\n<p><b>The Big Idea.</b> Two components store energy in a circuit and react to\n<b>change</b>: the capacitor and the inductor.</p>\n<h3>Capacitor — stores a charge</h3>\n<p>A <b>capacitor</b> is two plates separated by an insulator (the\n<b>dielectric</b>). It stores energy in an <b>electric field</b> and\n<b>opposes a change in VOLTAGE</b> (it charges and discharges). Capacitance is\nmeasured in <b>farads (F)</b> — usually microfarads (µF) or picofarads.\n<b>Trap:</b> a capacitor <b>blocks DC</b> (once charged, no more flows) but\n<b>passes AC</b>.</p>\n<h3>Inductor — stores in a magnetic field</h3>\n<p>An <b>inductor</b> is a coil. It stores energy in a <b>magnetic field</b> and\n<b>opposes a change in CURRENT</b>. Inductance is measured in <b>henries (H)</b>.\nIt's the mirror image of the capacitor: an inductor <b>passes DC</b> easily but\n<b>resists AC</b>.</p>\n<div class=\"box ex\"><span class=\"bt\">Memory hook</span>\n<b>C</b>apacitor opposes <b>V</b>oltage change and blocks DC.\n<b>L</b> (inductor) opposes current change and blocks AC.\n\"ELI the ICE man\": in an <b>L</b>, <b>E</b> leads <b>I</b>; in a <b>C</b>,\n<b>I</b> leads <b>E</b>.</div>\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">Must-Know</span>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Capacitor:</b> stores charge in an electric field; opposes <b>voltage</b> change; <b>blocks DC, passes AC</b>; unit = <b>farad</b>.</li>\n<li><b>Inductor:</b> stores energy in a magnetic field; opposes <b>current</b> change; <b>passes DC, resists AC</b>; unit = <b>henry</b>.</li>\n<li><b>ELI the ICE man</b> — voltage/current lead-lag in inductors vs. capacitors.</li>\n</ul></div>\n<p class=\"cite\">Source: FAA-H-8083-30B, Chapter 12 (Capacitance and inductance).</p>\n", "quiz": [{"q": "The basic unit of inductance is the:", "options": ["Ohm", "Henry", "Farad", "Coulomb"], "correct": 1, "short": "Henry", "expl": "The henry (H) is the basic unit of inductance. A circuit has one henry when current changing at one ampere per second induces one volt.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-58", "topic": "Capacitance & Inductance"}, {"q": "Which capacitor type is generally POLARIZED (marked + and -) and should be used only with DC or pulsating DC?", "options": ["Air capacitor", "Ceramic capacitor", "Electrolytic capacitor", "Mica capacitor"], "correct": 2, "short": "Electrolytic capacitor", "expl": "Electrolytic capacitors are generally polarized (+ and - leads marked) and should be subjected to direct or pulsating direct voltage only. They provide large capacitance in small size.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-52", "topic": "Capacitance & Inductance"}, {"q": "The two more common practical units of capacitance, smaller than the farad, are the microfarad and the:", "options": ["Henry", "Picofarad", "Kilofarad", "Megafarad"], "correct": 1, "short": "Picofarad", "expl": "Because one farad is large, smaller units are used: the microfarad (10^-6 F) and the picofarad (10^-12 F).", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-51", "topic": "Capacitance & Inductance"}, {"q": "One farad is defined as one coulomb of charge stored with how much voltage across the plates?", "options": ["Ten volts", "One hundred volts", "One volt", "One thousand volts"], "correct": 2, "short": "One volt", "expl": "By definition, one farad is one coulomb of charge stored with one volt across the plates of the capacitor (C = Q/E).", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-50", "topic": "Capacitance & Inductance"}, {"q": "In a series RL circuit, the current increases to what percentage of full value in 1 time constant after the circuit is closed?", "options": ["50 percent", "63 percent", "99 percent", "86 percent"], "correct": 1, "short": "63 percent", "expl": "In a series RL circuit, current increases to 63 percent of full value in 1 time constant, and reaches 99 percent after the 5th time constant — like an RC charging curve.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-56", "topic": "Capacitance & Inductance"}, {"q": "A capacitor reaches approximately 99 percent of full charge after how many RC time constants?", "options": ["2", "3", "1", "5"], "correct": 3, "short": "5 time constants", "expl": "The time to reach about 99 percent of source voltage is approximately 5 time constants (5 tau).", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-50", "topic": "Capacitance & Inductance"}, {"q": "When inductors are connected in PARALLEL, the total inductance is:", "options": ["Equal to the largest", "The product of the inductors", "The sum of the inductors", "Less than the smallest inductance"], "correct": 3, "short": "Less than the smallest", "expl": "When inductors are connected in parallel, the total inductance is less than the smallest inductance (uses the reciprocal formula, like resistors in parallel).", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-58", "topic": "Capacitance & Inductance"}, {"q": "Capacitors connected in PARALLEL combine how (to find total capacitance)?", "options": ["Use the reciprocal formula", "Take the smallest value", "Add them directly (CT = C1 + C2 + ...)", "Take the product over sum"], "correct": 2, "short": "Add them directly", "expl": "Capacitors in parallel add directly: CT = C1 + C2 + C3... (the effective plate area increases). This is OPPOSITE to resistors in parallel — a common trap.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-53", "topic": "Capacitance & Inductance"}, {"q": "Faraday's Law (the Law of Electromagnetic Induction) states the induced emf in a closed loop of wire is proportional to:", "options": ["The wire color", "The rate of change of the magnetic flux through the coil", "The applied DC voltage", "The wire resistance"], "correct": 1, "short": "Rate of change of magnetic flux", "expl": "Faraday's Law: the induced emf in a closed loop of wire is proportional to the rate of change of the magnetic flux through a coil of wire.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-56", "topic": "Capacitance & Inductance"}]}, {"id": "b4m4s2", "title": "Reactance, impedance & power factor", "lesson": "\n<p><b>The Big Idea.</b> In AC circuits, capacitors and inductors act like\nresistance — but it's called <b>reactance</b> because it depends on\n<b>frequency</b>.</p>\n<h3>Reactance</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Inductive reactance (X_L)</b> goes <b>UP</b> as frequency goes up (an inductor\nfights fast changes harder).</li>\n<li><b>Capacitive reactance (X_C)</b> goes <b>DOWN</b> as frequency goes up (a\ncapacitor passes high frequencies more easily).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Impedance</h3>\n<p><b>Impedance (Z)</b> is the TOTAL opposition to AC — resistance and reactance\ncombined, measured in <b>ohms</b>. It's the AC version of resistance and it's what\nOhm's Law uses in an AC circuit (E = I × Z).</p>\n<h3>Power factor</h3>\n<p>In AC, voltage and current can get out of step. <b>Power factor</b> = true power\n÷ apparent power (a number from 0 to 1). <b>Perfectly resistive = 1.0.</b>\n<b>True power</b> (watts) is the real work; <b>apparent power</b> (volt-amps, VA)\nis voltage × current before accounting for the phase difference.</p>\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">Must-Know</span>\n<ul>\n<li><b>X_L rises</b> with frequency; <b>X_C falls</b> with frequency.</li>\n<li><b>Impedance (Z)</b> = total AC opposition (R + reactance), in ohms.</li>\n<li><b>Power factor</b> = true power ÷ apparent power; <b>1.0 = purely resistive</b>.</li>\n</ul></div>\n<p class=\"cite\">Source: FAA-H-8083-30B, Chapter 12 (Reactance, impedance, power factor).</p>\n", "quiz": [{"q": "For a series RLC circuit with R = 4 ohms, XL = 10 ohms, XC = 7 ohms, the impedance Z is:", "options": ["3 ohms", "21 ohms", "5 ohms", "11 ohms"], "correct": 2, "short": "5 ohms", "expl": "Z = sqrt(R^2 + (XL - XC)^2) = sqrt(4^2 + (10-7)^2) = sqrt(16 + 9) = sqrt(25) = 5 ohms. Handbook worked example.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-62", "topic": "Impedance & Power Factor"}, {"q": "Besides resistance, which TWO factors oppose current flow ONLY in AC circuits (not DC)?", "options": ["Voltage and current", "Inductive reactance and capacitive reactance", "Permeability and reluctance", "Impedance and resistance"], "correct": 1, "short": "Inductive & capacitive reactance", "expl": "Resistance opposes both AC and DC. Inductive reactance (XL) and capacitive reactance (XC) oppose current only in AC circuits and can create a phase shift.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-49", "topic": "AC Reactance"}, {"q": "The combined total opposition to current flow in an AC circuit, made up of resistance and reactance, is called:", "options": ["Conductance", "Capacitance", "Inductance", "Impedance"], "correct": 3, "short": "Impedance", "expl": "The combined effects of resistance, inductive reactance, and capacitive reactance make up the total opposition called impedance (Z), measured in ohms.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-59", "topic": "Impedance & Power Factor"}, {"q": "A 220-volt AC motor draws 50 amperes; a wattmeter reads 9,350 watts. What is the apparent power?", "options": ["11,000 volt-amperes", "9,350 watts", "270 VA", "4,675 VA"], "correct": 0, "short": "11,000 volt-amperes", "expl": "Apparent power = volts x amperes = 220 x 50 = 11,000 watts (volt-amperes). This is the handbook's worked example.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-65", "topic": "Impedance & Power Factor"}, {"q": "Ohm's Law for an AC circuit is written as:", "options": ["I = E / R", "I = E x Z", "I = E / Z", "I = Z / E"], "correct": 2, "short": "I = E / Z", "expl": "For AC, Ohm's Law uses impedance: I = E/Z. When the circuit is resistance only, Z = R and it is the same as the DC form.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-59", "topic": "Impedance & Power Factor"}, {"q": "The ratio of true power to apparent power, usually expressed as a percent, is called the:", "options": ["Power factor", "Efficiency", "Coefficient of coupling", "Turns ratio"], "correct": 0, "short": "Power factor", "expl": "Power factor (PF) is the ratio of true power to apparent power, usually expressed in percent: PF = (watts / volt-amperes) x 100.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-64", "topic": "Impedance & Power Factor"}, {"q": "In the handbook example, an inductance of 0.146 H at 60 cps gives an inductive reactance (XL = 2 pi f L) of approximately:", "options": ["11 ohms", "110 ohms", "55 ohms", "8 ohms"], "correct": 2, "short": "55 ohms", "expl": "XL = 6.28 x 60 x 0.146 = 55 ohms. This is the handbook's worked example.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-58", "topic": "AC Reactance"}, {"q": "When a circuit contains resistance, inductance, AND capacitance, the reactances are combined by:", "options": ["Multiplying XL and XC", "Adding XL and XC", "Ignoring the smaller one", "Subtracting the smaller reactance from the larger (X = XL - XC)"], "correct": 3, "short": "Subtracting (X = XL - XC)", "expl": "Because their effects are exactly opposite, inductive and capacitive reactance are combined by subtraction (smaller from larger): then Z = sqrt(R^2 + (XL - XC)^2).", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-60", "topic": "Impedance & Power Factor"}, {"q": "The power consumed by the ENTIRE AC circuit (both resistance and reactance), measured in volt-amps, is called:", "options": ["True power", "Working power", "Real power", "Apparent power"], "correct": 3, "short": "Apparent power", "expl": "Apparent power (symbol S, measured in volt-amps) is the power consumed by the entire circuit, including resistance and reactance — the product of effective voltage and current.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-64", "topic": "Impedance & Power Factor"}]}, {"id": "b4m4s3", "title": "Transformers, motors & generators", "lesson": "\n<p><b>The Big Idea.</b> Coils and magnets turn electricity into motion, motion into\nelectricity, and one AC voltage into another.</p>\n<h3>Transformers</h3>\n<p>A <b>transformer</b> changes AC voltage up or down using two coils sharing a\nmagnetic core. <b>Trap:</b> transformers work on <b>AC ONLY</b> (they need a\nchanging field). More turns on the secondary → higher voltage (<b>step-up</b>);\nfewer turns → <b>step-down</b>. The voltage ratio equals the <b>turns ratio</b>.\nPower in ≈ power out, so if voltage goes up, current goes down.</p>\n<h3>Generators &amp; motors</h3>\n<p>A <b>generator</b> spins a coil in a magnetic field to <b>make</b> electricity\n(motion → current). A <b>motor</b> is the same machine run backward:\ncurrent → motion. A <b>DC generator</b> uses a <b>commutator</b> (a split ring) to\nkeep the output flowing one way; an <b>alternator</b> makes AC and uses\n<b>slip rings</b>. Modern aircraft mostly use alternators because they make more\ncurrent at low RPM and weigh less.</p>\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">Must-Know</span>\n<ul>\n<li>Transformers work on <b>AC only</b>; voltage ratio = <b>turns ratio</b>; step-up adds turns.</li>\n<li><b>Generator:</b> motion → electricity. <b>Motor:</b> electricity → motion.</li>\n<li>DC generator uses a <b>commutator</b>; an <b>alternator</b> makes AC with slip rings.</li>\n</ul></div>\n<p class=\"cite\">Source: FAA-H-8083-30B, Chapter 12 (Transformers, motors, generators).</p>\n", "quiz": [{"q": "No transformer is 100 percent efficient because some of the magnetic flux escapes the magnetic circuit. This lost flux is called:", "options": ["Hysteresis flux", "Mutual flux", "Leakage flux", "Eddy flux"], "correct": 2, "short": "Leakage flux", "expl": "A certain amount of magnetic flux, called leakage flux, leaks out of the magnetic circuit; thus no transformer is 100 percent efficient (though iron-core types approach it).", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-66", "topic": "Transformers"}, {"q": "As a DC motor's armature speed INCREASES, the counter emf increases and the current through the armature:", "options": ["Increases", "Reverses", "Decreases", "Stays the same"], "correct": 2, "short": "Decreases", "expl": "The faster the armature rotates, the greater the counter emf; counter emf opposes the applied voltage, so armature current decreases as speed increases. A motor draws high current on starting.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-142", "topic": "Motors & Generators"}, {"q": "A DC motor that combines a series winding and a shunt winding in the field is a:", "options": ["Synchronous motor", "Shunt motor", "Series motor", "Compound motor"], "correct": 3, "short": "Compound motor", "expl": "The compound motor combines series and shunt motors; it has both a shunt winding and a series winding in the field. Its starting torque is higher than a shunt but lower than a series motor.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-142", "topic": "Motors & Generators"}, {"q": "What is the danger if a SERIES motor has its load removed entirely?", "options": ["It draws no current", "It stalls immediately", "It may overspeed until the armature flies apart", "It reverses direction"], "correct": 2, "short": "It may overspeed and fly apart", "expl": "A series motor runs at high speed under light load; if the load is removed entirely, the motor may operate at such a high speed that the armature flies apart.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-142", "topic": "Motors & Generators"}, {"q": "The frequency of an alternator's output voltage depends on the number of poles and the:", "options": ["Voltage applied", "Speed of rotation of the rotor", "Core material", "Wire gauge"], "correct": 1, "short": "Speed of rotation", "expl": "Alternator frequency depends on the number of poles and the speed of rotation: the faster the speed (or the more poles), the higher the frequency.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-157", "topic": "Motors & Generators"}, {"q": "The factor that determines whether a transformer is step-up or step-down is the:", "options": ["Core material", "Turns ratio", "Wire gauge", "Frequency"], "correct": 1, "short": "Turns ratio", "expl": "The turns ratio — primary turns to secondary turns — determines step-up vs. step-down. The voltage ratio equals the turns ratio in an ideal transformer.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-65", "topic": "Transformers"}, {"q": "Aircraft inverters are usually built to supply AC current at a frequency of:", "options": ["1,000 cps", "60 cps", "400 cps", "50 cps"], "correct": 2, "short": "400 cps", "expl": "Inverters are usually built to supply current at a frequency of 400 cps. Some provide more than one voltage (e.g., 26 V AC and 115 V AC).", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-93", "topic": "Motors & Generators"}, {"q": "A step-down transformer with a 5-to-1 turns ratio, fed with 10 volts on the primary, produces what secondary voltage?", "options": ["5 volts", "50 volts", "10 volts", "2 volts"], "correct": 3, "short": "2 volts", "expl": "Voltage ratio = turns ratio. A 5:1 step-down with 10 V on the primary gives 10/5 = 2 V on the secondary. Handbook example.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-65", "topic": "Transformers"}, {"q": "A transformer's primary develops 10,000 lines of force but only 9,000 cut the secondary. The transformer efficiency (coefficient of coupling) is:", "options": ["100 percent", "90 percent", "80 percent", "10 percent"], "correct": 1, "short": "90 percent", "expl": "9,000 / 10,000 = 0.9, so the coefficient of coupling is 0.9 and the transformer is 90 percent efficient.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-66", "topic": "Transformers"}]}, {"id": "b4m4s4", "title": "Semiconductors: diodes, transistors & logic", "lesson": "\n<p><b>The Big Idea.</b> <b>Semiconductors</b> (mostly <b>silicon</b>) are materials\nin between conductors and insulators. By adding tiny impurities (<b>doping</b>) we\nmake <b>N-type</b> (extra electrons) and <b>P-type</b> (extra \"holes\") material —\nthe building blocks of all modern electronics.</p>\n<h3>Diode — the one-way valve</h3>\n<p>A <b>diode</b> is one P-N junction. It lets current flow <b>one way only</b>\n(like a check valve). <b>Forward-biased</b> = conducts; <b>reverse-biased</b> =\nblocks. The main job is <b>rectification</b> — turning AC into DC. An\n<b>LED</b> is a diode that emits light when it conducts.</p>\n<h3>Transistor — switch and amplifier</h3>\n<p>A <b>transistor</b> has two junctions and three leads (<b>emitter, base,\ncollector</b>). A tiny current at the <b>base</b> controls a much bigger current —\nso a transistor can <b>amplify</b> a signal or act as a fast <b>switch</b>. Millions\nof them etched onto a chip make an <b>integrated circuit</b>.</p>\n<h3>Logic circuits</h3>\n<p>Digital electronics use <b>logic gates</b> — AND, OR, NOT — that act on 1s and 0s\n(on/off). An <b>AND</b> gate outputs 1 only if all inputs are 1; an <b>OR</b> gate\noutputs 1 if any input is 1; a <b>NOT</b> gate inverts.</p>\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">Must-Know</span>\n<ul>\n<li>Semiconductor = silicon; <b>doping</b> makes <b>N-type</b> (electrons) and <b>P-type</b> (holes).</li>\n<li><b>Diode</b> = one-way valve; main use = <b>rectification</b> (AC → DC).</li>\n<li><b>Transistor</b> leads = <b>emitter, base, collector</b>; small base current controls a big current (amplify/switch).</li>\n</ul></div>\n<p class=\"cite\">Source: FAA-H-8083-30B, Chapter 12 (Semiconductors, diodes, transistors, logic).</p>\n", "quiz": [{"q": "A logic gate whose output is HIGH when ANY ONE (or more) of its inputs is high is the:", "options": ["AND gate", "OR gate", "Inverter", "NAND gate"], "correct": 1, "short": "OR gate", "expl": "The OR gate output is high whenever any one of its inputs is high; the output is low only when no input is high.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-121", "topic": "Logic Circuits"}, {"q": "The name 'transistor' is a combination of which two words?", "options": ["Transit and register", "Transform and resistor", "Transmit and resistor", "Transfer and resistor"], "correct": 3, "short": "Transfer and resistor", "expl": "Because the device transfers current from a low-resistance path to a high-resistance one, its name combines 'transfer' and 'resistor' — transistor.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-107", "topic": "Transistors"}, {"q": "A logic gate whose output is HIGH only when ALL of its inputs are high is the:", "options": ["NOT gate", "NOR gate", "OR gate", "AND gate"], "correct": 3, "short": "AND gate", "expl": "The AND gate output is high only when all inputs are high. If any input is low, the output is low — it determines when conditions are met at the same time.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-121", "topic": "Logic Circuits"}, {"q": "A semiconductor doped with a donor impurity (such as phosphorus or arsenic) that produces an EXCESS of electrons is called:", "options": ["Neutral-type", "Intrinsic", "P-type", "N-type"], "correct": 3, "short": "N-type", "expl": "An N-type semiconductor is doped with a donor impurity, producing an excess of electrons; the electrons are the majority current carriers.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-96", "topic": "Semiconductors & Diodes"}, {"q": "To use a transistor as an amplifier, the emitter-base junction is biased in the forward direction (low resistance) and the collector-base junction is:", "options": ["Left unbiased", "Forward biased (low resistance)", "Reverse biased (high resistance)", "Shorted"], "correct": 2, "short": "Reverse biased (high resistance)", "expl": "For amplification, the emitter-base junction is forward biased (low resistance) and the collector-base junction is reverse biased (high resistance), producing power gain.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-106", "topic": "Transistors"}, {"q": "Transistors are classified as either NPN or PNP according to:", "options": ["The arrangement of their N and P materials", "The number of leads", "Their color code", "Their power rating"], "correct": 0, "short": "Arrangement of N and P materials", "expl": "Transistors are classified NPN or PNP according to the arrangement of their N and P materials. NPN has P sandwiched between two N regions; PNP is the opposite.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-105", "topic": "Transistors"}, {"q": "A logic gate (also called a NOT gate) that converts one logic state into its opposite is the:", "options": ["OR gate", "Inverter", "NAND gate", "AND gate"], "correct": 1, "short": "Inverter (NOT gate)", "expl": "The inverter (NOT gate) converts one logic state into the opposite — a 1 to a 0 or a 0 to a 1.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-121", "topic": "Logic Circuits"}, {"q": "A rectifier that produces output from only one half (e.g., the positive swing) of each AC cycle is a:", "options": ["Half-wave rectifier", "Full-wave rectifier", "Zener rectifier", "Bridge rectifier"], "correct": 0, "short": "Half-wave rectifier", "expl": "The half-wave rectifier reproduces the input minus the negative voltage swing — it uses only one half of each cycle.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-101", "topic": "Semiconductors & Diodes"}, {"q": "In a forward-biased diode where energy is dissipated as LIGHT rather than heat, the device is a:", "options": ["Light-emitting diode (LED)", "Zener diode", "Varactor", "Photodiode"], "correct": 0, "short": "Light-emitting diode (LED)", "expl": "In an LED, the energy released as electrons fall into holes is dissipated as light. Advantages over incandescent lamps: longer life, lower voltage, faster operation, less heat.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-103", "topic": "Semiconductors & Diodes"}]}]}]};
